content.cockpit_and_administration:
One of the most consequential architectural changes in openSUSE Leap 16.0 is the transition from YaST to Cockpit for manual system administration.

Historically, SUSE and openSUSE distributions were defined by YaST (Yet another Setup Tool), an extensive administration suite providing ncurses text-mode and Qt graphical modules for configuring network interfaces, storage partitions, user accounts, and system services. In Leap 16.0, Cockpit — a browser-based, lightweight system administration interface that communicates directly with systemd, DBus, and standard Linux APIs — has become the primary administration tool.

To support this shift, openSUSE developers created and enhanced dedicated Cockpit modules:

cockpit-subscriptions manages registration with SUSE Linux Enterprise for systems transitioning to commercial support;
cockpit-repositories allows administrators to view, add, modify, and refresh package sources directly from the browser;
cockpit-packages provides package search and software installation without requiring terminal execution;
cockpit-packagekit offers selective update management, allowing individual package updates to be selected or deferred.

These custom modules join upstream Cockpit modules for network management, storage volume configuration, virtual machine oversight, and container orchestration. The release notes acknowledge that this transition is evolving, with certain advanced YaST modules still maintained for specialised setups.
